{
    "background_story": "In a small village nestled in the rolling hills of rural India, there lived a majestic cow named Jujube. She was a sacred Brahma cow, revered for her gentle nature and majestic appearance. The villagers believed that Jujube possessed a spark of the divine, and they would often offer her flowers and prayers. According to local legend, Jujube was a reincarnation of the goddess Lakshmi, and her presence brought prosperity and good fortune to the village. As a symbol of Brahmanism, Jujube embodied the principles of ahimsa (non-violence) and karuna (compassion). She would often wander into the village, and the children would delight in feeding her and playing with her soft ears. Jujube's calm demeanor and gentle lowing seemed to bring peace to all who surrounded her, reminding the villagers of the importance of living in harmony with nature and respecting all living beings.",
